WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.570 --> 00:00:05.560
More than any other comic book hero, Superman
has been as defined by his portrayals off

00:00:05.560 --> 00:00:07.200
the page as he is within them.

00:00:07.440 --> 00:00:12.579
Whether it's the radio show introducing Kryptonite
and Jimmy Olsen in the '40s or the way Christopher

00:00:12.579 --> 00:00:17.710
Reeve made audiences believe a man could fly
in 1978, the men playing the Man of Steel

00:00:17.710 --> 00:00:19.540
have shaped how the world views Superman.

00:00:19.960 --> 00:00:21.380
But which one did it best?

00:00:21.640 --> 00:00:25.860
Here's a look at every live-action version
of Superman ranked from worst to best.

00:00:27.020 --> 00:00:28.020
Superman Returns

00:00:28.760 --> 00:00:33.860
2006's Superman Returns isn't just terrible,
it's the kind of terrible that makes you wonder

00:00:33.860 --> 00:00:34.660
how it happened.

00:00:34.880 --> 00:00:39.020
It's mystifying on every level, starting with
the fact that it's meant to be a sequel to

00:00:39.030 --> 00:00:44.760
the director's cut of Superman II not the
theatrical release, but Richard Donner's original

00:00:44.760 --> 00:00:48.040
version, which had only been released on DVD
a few years earlier.

00:00:48.360 --> 00:00:53.580
That's weird, especially considering it ignores
the two sequels to Superman II that already

00:00:53.580 --> 00:00:54.080
existed.

00:00:54.500 --> 00:00:59.320
Worse, though, the version of Superman that
the movie presents us is remarkably unappealing.

00:00:59.380 --> 00:01:04.420
After leaving Earth for five years to check
and see if Krypton was still blown up, Superman

00:01:04.430 --> 00:01:08.540
returns to find that Lois Lane has been spending
the intervening years raising their five-year-old

00:01:08.540 --> 00:01:09.040
child.

00:01:09.280 --> 00:01:13.640
Superman then mopes around, basically stalking
his ex-girlfriend for two hours.

00:01:14.140 --> 00:01:18.130
The most frustrating part about the whole
thing is that Brandon Routh is actually good

00:01:18.130 --> 00:01:18.660
in the role.

00:01:18.920 --> 00:01:24.100
He's got a great look, he comes off as genuine
and heroic, and he's incredibly likable he

00:01:24.100 --> 00:01:27.960
just happens to be playing a version of Superman
that's none of those things.

00:01:28.220 --> 00:01:29.300
It's a Bird...

00:01:29.400 --> 00:01:30.300
It's a Plane...

00:01:30.700 --> 00:01:31.700
It's Superman

00:01:32.000 --> 00:01:34.260
"Hi there America and friendly nations everywhere.

00:01:34.740 --> 00:01:35.820
Glad we could get together."

00:01:35.920 --> 00:01:42.200
This 1975 TV adaptation of the popular '60s
Broadway musical taps into the same campy

00:01:42.200 --> 00:01:45.340
vibe that made the Batman TV show such a big
hit.

00:01:45.560 --> 00:01:48.200
That actually led to some pretty cool stuff,
too.

00:01:48.200 --> 00:01:52.600
The sets being drawn as flat, comic book-style
backgrounds for the actors to play against

00:01:52.600 --> 00:01:57.500
complete with panel borders drawn in was a
great idea, and there are a few really well-done

00:01:57.500 --> 00:01:58.000
gags.

00:01:58.140 --> 00:02:02.660
Plus, "You've Got Possibilities," the song
in which a vampy femme fatale swings a meek

00:02:02.660 --> 00:02:07.940
Clark Kent around the room while undressing
him is… something else.

00:02:07.940 --> 00:02:13.080
In practice, though, it just doesn't come
together, thanks in large part to David Wilson's

00:02:13.080 --> 00:02:13.760
Superman.

00:02:13.920 --> 00:02:18.100
Like almost everyone else in the cast, he
feels like he's constantly winking at the

00:02:18.100 --> 00:02:20.880
audience about how goofy this entire thing
is.

00:02:21.640 --> 00:02:23.380
"Yeah, I know."

00:02:23.480 --> 00:02:28.360
He plays Clark Kent just a little too weedy
and bored, and his Superman is a little too

00:02:28.360 --> 00:02:31.080
much of a blowhard to ever really want to
see him win.

00:02:32.460 --> 00:02:33.460
Superboy

00:02:33.940 --> 00:02:39.380
Before Smallville managed to carve out a solid
decade on the airwaves, the story of a younger,

00:02:39.380 --> 00:02:44.600
pre-Superman Clark Kent found its way to syndicated
television as Superboy in 1988.

00:02:44.780 --> 00:02:49.120
Set during Clark's college years in Florida,
the first season was shot on a tight budget,

00:02:49.120 --> 00:02:52.800
but still managed to tell pretty interesting
stories about its title character, played

00:02:52.800 --> 00:02:54.400
by John Haymes Newton.

00:02:54.520 --> 00:03:00.100
Though the series was a hit, Newton only lasted
a single season, after producers used a DUI

00:03:00.120 --> 00:03:02.960
as an excuse to replace him after he asked
for a pay raise.

00:03:03.180 --> 00:03:06.980
"This imposter will be more than happy to
sign a full confession to the crimes committed

00:03:07.000 --> 00:03:07.780
in my name."

00:03:08.700 --> 00:03:09.700
Superman

00:03:10.280 --> 00:03:16.100
Here's the weirdest thing about Kirk Alyn's
tenure as Superman in the 1948 serial: while

00:03:16.100 --> 00:03:21.040
his name did appear on the posters, identifying
him as the actor who played Clark Kent, Columbia

00:03:21.040 --> 00:03:24.080
Pictures never actually credited him in the
serial itself.

00:03:24.240 --> 00:03:29.120
Instead, they promoted the film as starring
Superman, taking some time off from fighting

00:03:29.120 --> 00:03:32.240
crime and saving the world to make his motion
picture debut.

00:03:32.540 --> 00:03:36.900
Looking back, it seems like a pretty bizarre
promotional choice, but in all honesty, Alyn

00:03:36.900 --> 00:03:37.900
really did sell it.

00:03:38.180 --> 00:03:42.460
His big smile and hell-raiser attitude were
a great match for the early iteration of the

00:03:42.460 --> 00:03:43.040
character.

00:03:43.200 --> 00:03:47.140
There was one truly bizarre thing about the
serial version of Superman, though: since

00:03:47.260 --> 00:03:51.320
they didn't have the budget for a live-action
special effect, all the scenes of Superman

00:03:51.320 --> 00:03:57.640
flying were done by showing Alyn's very human
form quickly turning into an animated cartoon

00:03:57.640 --> 00:04:01.700
that would fly around before landing behind
something, so they wouldn't have to animate

00:04:01.780 --> 00:04:02.280
the landing.

00:04:02.600 --> 00:04:03.640
That's weird!

00:04:04.480 --> 00:04:06.040
Süpermen Dönüyor

00:04:06.220 --> 00:04:11.500
Is Turkey's 1979 bootleg action epic The Return
of Superman good?

00:04:11.620 --> 00:04:12.120
No.

00:04:12.520 --> 00:04:13.180
No it is not.

00:04:13.700 --> 00:04:18.440
Is it, however, absolutely worth watching,
if only to see how truly insane it is?

00:04:19.080 --> 00:04:19.580
Yes.

00:04:19.920 --> 00:04:24.740
Since Turkish laws at the time prevented American
blockbusters like Superman from being shown,

00:04:24.840 --> 00:04:28.060
enterprising Turkish filmmakers just made
their own version.

00:04:28.060 --> 00:04:33.040
This resulted in a film in which a suspiciously
scrawny Superman comes to Earth from a Krypton

00:04:33.040 --> 00:04:37.020
that appears to be decorated by Christmas
tree ornaments, and the shots of Superman

00:04:37.060 --> 00:04:42.780
flying are done by dangling a Ken doll in
a homemade Superman costume on a string.

00:04:42.820 --> 00:04:49.200
So no, it's not what you'd call good, but
Tayfun Demir's performance is certainly unique,

00:04:49.200 --> 00:04:50.740
and incredibly entertaining.

00:04:51.380 --> 00:04:53.320
The Adventures of Superboy

00:04:53.560 --> 00:04:57.860
After John Newton left the Superboy television
show, the title role for the second, third,

00:04:57.860 --> 00:05:02.400
and fourth seasons passed to Gerard Christopher,
who benefited from joining a show that was

00:05:02.420 --> 00:05:03.920
already a success.

00:05:04.020 --> 00:05:09.040
As a result, he had a lot more to do than
his predecessor, both on and offscreen Christopher

00:05:09.040 --> 00:05:11.700
actually ended up writing two episodes of
the series himself.

00:05:12.440 --> 00:05:14.900
"I want superboy!"

00:05:15.020 --> 00:05:18.660
He also had the benefit of being a little
older and having a little more experience.

00:05:18.920 --> 00:05:22.600
Despite the fact that he was playing a sophomore
in college, Christopher turned 30 the year

00:05:22.600 --> 00:05:26.180
he landed the role, and the experience he
brought to the table shows.

00:05:26.560 --> 00:05:30.920
There's a sincerity to his portrayal of Superboy,
and his Clark Kent was purposefully a little

00:05:30.980 --> 00:05:35.200
nerdier than Newton's, to bring him closer
into step with Christopher Reeve's bumbling,

00:05:35.200 --> 00:05:36.640
stuttering Clark from the films.

00:05:37.980 --> 00:05:38.800
Smallville

00:05:39.500 --> 00:05:45.380
With 218 episodes over the course of ten seasons
beginning in 2001, Smallville's Tom Welling

00:05:45.380 --> 00:05:49.480
put more time into playing Clark Kent than
any other live-action performer.

00:05:49.540 --> 00:05:51.520
But then, that's also the problem.

00:05:51.760 --> 00:05:56.600
Smallville famously had an approach rooted
in the simple rule: "no tights, no flights."

00:05:56.880 --> 00:06:00.620
The idea was that their focus would be on
a young Clark Kent in the years before he

00:06:00.620 --> 00:06:02.380
decided to become Superman.

00:06:02.540 --> 00:06:06.600
The show was such a hit, though, that it took
him a whole decade to get around to becoming

00:06:06.600 --> 00:06:07.120
Superman.

00:06:07.500 --> 00:06:12.180
As a result, Smallville's Clark became a victim
of his own success, forced by the rules of

00:06:12.180 --> 00:06:16.300
the show into an indecisiveness that even
Hamlet would find a little annoying.

00:06:16.880 --> 00:06:20.980
The show went on long enough that it even
outlasted the original setting of the title,

00:06:20.980 --> 00:06:24.760
and Clark ended up doing pretty much everything
you'd expect to see a grown-up Superman do.

00:06:24.940 --> 00:06:28.000
The thing is, he only did it as "the Red-Blue
Blur."

00:06:28.200 --> 00:06:32.520
If we ever get around to ranking Red-Blue
Blurs, he'll be at the top of the list, but

00:06:32.520 --> 00:06:34.800
as Superman, he leaves a little to be desired.

00:06:35.420 --> 00:06:38.080
Lois &amp; Clark: The New Adventures of Superman

00:06:38.200 --> 00:06:42.820
Smallville erred on the side of putting too
much focus on Clark Kent, and the DC's blockbusters

00:06:42.820 --> 00:06:46.980
went to the other extreme and barely bothered
to include Superman's alter ego at all.

00:06:47.280 --> 00:06:52.140
But beginning in 1993, Lois &amp; Clark struck
a balance that few other portrayals of the

00:06:52.140 --> 00:06:54.700
character have managed to even come close
to.

00:06:54.920 --> 00:06:59.320
The trick is right there in the title "Lois
&amp; Clark" comes first, emphasizing the love

00:06:59.320 --> 00:06:59.820
story.

00:06:59.880 --> 00:07:01.280
And this didn't detract from the action.

00:07:01.600 --> 00:07:06.020
Instead, it did the opposite, adding a level
of drama that got fans invested in a kind

00:07:06.020 --> 00:07:10.600
of ongoing soap opera without ever letting
it get in the way of super-powered action.

00:07:10.740 --> 00:07:14.940
The single best thing that Lois &amp; Clark ever
did was, after three different fake-outs,

00:07:14.940 --> 00:07:18.740
calling the episode where the title characters
got married, "Swear To God, This Time We're

00:07:18.740 --> 00:07:19.900
Not Kidding."

00:07:20.100 --> 00:07:23.920
That said, a close second was the way that
Dean Cain's take on the Man of Steel got the

00:07:23.920 --> 00:07:29.560
dynamic of the secret identity right: Superman
is what he does, Clark Kent is who he is.

00:07:29.560 --> 00:07:32.480
"That's ridiculous you are Superman."

00:07:32.500 --> 00:07:34.980
No, Superman is what I can do.

00:07:35.940 --> 00:07:37.800
Clark is who I am."

00:07:38.100 --> 00:07:38.800
And a close third?

00:07:39.080 --> 00:07:40.800
"Hey, hey, look over there!"

00:07:49.300 --> 00:07:51.860
Man of Steel and the DC Extended Universe

00:07:52.100 --> 00:07:58.060
The DCEU may have its origins in an unrelentingly
bleak place, but Henry Cavill's arc as Superman

00:07:58.060 --> 00:08:00.340
transcends even the dreariest parts.

00:08:00.540 --> 00:08:05.140
As Superman, he's pretty much the truest thing
you'll ever get to his comic appearance, and

00:08:05.140 --> 00:08:11.100
for the first time on screen, he's not just
a dude with superpowers, but an alien god.

00:08:11.380 --> 00:08:16.500
Sure, his initial arc consists of murdering
the villain, getting his butt whupped by Batman,

00:08:16.500 --> 00:08:21.100
and then dying pretty dark, but also a great
match for Ben Affleck's Batman.

00:08:21.220 --> 00:08:27.220
By the end of 2017's Justice League, we got
a sunnier and more optimistic version of Superman:

00:08:27.240 --> 00:08:31.920
a guy who could joke around with The Flash,
and hopefully open the door to a much brighter

00:08:32.020 --> 00:08:32.720
DCEU.

00:08:32.920 --> 00:08:34.940
What more could you want in a Superman?

00:08:35.920 --> 00:08:37.720
The Adventures of Superman

00:08:37.940 --> 00:08:42.520
Even though the show was on the air for over
a hundred episodes beginning in 1952, the

00:08:42.520 --> 00:08:46.900
most defining moment for George Reeves' tenure
as Superman on The Adventures of Superman

00:08:46.900 --> 00:08:48.580
didn't happen on the screen.

00:08:48.720 --> 00:08:50.060
It happened in real life.

00:08:50.260 --> 00:08:53.460
Reeves was acutely aware of the fact that
kids looked up to him.

00:08:53.880 --> 00:08:57.600
He even quit smoking so that he wouldn't be
caught by a photographer and set a bad example

00:08:57.600 --> 00:09:01.860
for his younger viewers, and pushed for scripts
that would give good messages about respect

00:09:01.860 --> 00:09:03.260
and tolerance of others.

00:09:03.480 --> 00:09:08.100
The downside to that was that when he made
public appearances in costume, a few children

00:09:08.100 --> 00:09:12.580
would attempt to test Superman's invulnerability
with a quick punch or a kick to the shins.

00:09:12.820 --> 00:09:16.980
At one appearance, a kid took things much
further by bringing his father's pistol in

00:09:16.980 --> 00:09:20.380
an effort to see if bullets really would bounce
off Superman.

00:09:20.600 --> 00:09:25.420
Reeves, thinking quickly, convinced the child
to hand over the loaded gun, as immortalized

00:09:25.420 --> 00:09:27.480
in the 2006 film Hollywoodland:

00:09:28.020 --> 00:09:33.380
"If you did shoot me, and the bullet bounced
off, it might accidentally hit someone else.

00:09:35.120 --> 00:09:36.060
We don't want that to happen, do we?"

00:09:36.480 --> 00:09:39.300
That's the kind of thing Superman himself
would have done.

00:09:40.420 --> 00:09:41.420
Supergirl

00:09:41.740 --> 00:09:46.220
There's a moment in the second season of Supergirl
where everyone is eagerly awaiting the arrival

00:09:46.220 --> 00:09:48.740
of Kara Zor-El's more famous cousin.

00:09:48.920 --> 00:09:53.360
When he does show up, Superman lands, and
then spends the next few minutes smiling and

00:09:53.360 --> 00:09:58.480
shaking hands with everyone, learning their
names and greeting them not as an alien savior,

00:09:58.480 --> 00:10:00.340
but someone who sees them as equals.

00:10:00.680 --> 00:10:04.240
That's the moment where it was clear that
the show's producers understood Superman as

00:10:04.240 --> 00:10:06.120
well as they understand his cousin.

00:10:06.380 --> 00:10:11.220
Tyler Hoechlin's take on Superman has a warmth
and honesty that goes perfectly with the world

00:10:11.220 --> 00:10:15.480
the show has created, an affable attitude
and humanity that doesn't go away when he

00:10:15.480 --> 00:10:16.900
takes off his glasses.

00:10:16.940 --> 00:10:21.580
With his first appearance in 2017, he might
not have the tenure of some of the other Supermen

00:10:21.580 --> 00:10:22.720
on this list.

00:10:22.900 --> 00:10:27.020
But what he does have is a note-perfect vision
of a hero who's here to help.

00:10:28.300 --> 00:10:29.300
Superman

00:10:29.740 --> 00:10:32.540
If you go looking for people's thoughts about
Christopher Reeve's performance as The Man

00:10:32.900 --> 00:10:37.800
of Steel starting in 1978's Superman, the
one thing you'll read over and over is that

00:10:37.800 --> 00:10:41.720
he looked like he stepped off the pages of
the comics and into the real world.

00:10:42.000 --> 00:10:46.440
But the truth is that Reeve embodied the idea
of Superman so well that the comics had to

00:10:46.440 --> 00:10:47.700
change to keep up.

00:10:47.820 --> 00:10:52.060
His easy smile, his reassuring confidence;
it's all there on screen.

00:10:52.360 --> 00:10:56.600
He even made the eye-popping costume look
great, and did such a great job with the double

00:10:56.600 --> 00:11:00.980
identity that it was easy to believe the citizens
of Metropolis would overlook the meek, slouchy

00:11:00.980 --> 00:11:03.640
Clark Kent as a disguise for Superman.

00:11:03.740 --> 00:11:08.480
Looking back at them years later, the movies
themselves can be wildly uneven 3 and 4 aren't

00:11:08.480 --> 00:11:11.460
as bad as you remember, and the first two
aren't as good

00:11:11.680 --> 00:11:16.260
but at least Reeve's performance remains unassailably
great in virtually every way.

00:11:17.620 --> 00:11:18.620
Thanks for watching!

